ptoms?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Adhesions"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Enteric nervous system damage"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Impacted stool"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Norovirus"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Twisting of the bowel"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Mesenteric venous thrombosis', 'Abnormal gastrointestinal motility'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.4}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Diabetic Gastroparesis", "posterior": 0.309, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1", "B2.2", "B2.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=dxs]\n\nStructural Bowel Obstruction vs Diabetic Gastroparesis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal swelling, abdominal tenderness, blood in stool\n  Favours second: feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, poor appetite, trouble controlling blood sugar, weight loss\n\nStructural Bowel Obstruction vs Adhesive Small Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal swelling, abdominal tenderness\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, constipation, failure to pass flatus\n\nDiabetic Gastroparesis vs Adhesive Small Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, poor appetite, trouble controlling blood sugar\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, blood in stool, constipation, dehydration, diarrhea\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: Development and psychometric evaluation of the Diabetic Gastroparesis Symptom Severity Diary. > History and Physical\n  Nausea is the most common symptom in gastroparesis. Other common symptoms include vomiting, early satiety, postprandial fullness, and bloating. Vomitus often contains undigested chewed food. Both weig\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Gastroparesis. > History and Physical\n  Gastroparesis is a heterogeneous disorder. Symptoms do not always correlate with the degree of delayed gastric emptying, and some patients positive for gastroparesis on diagnostic testing are asymptom\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Development and psychometric evaluation of the Diabetic Gastroparesis Symptom Severity Diary. > Evaluation\n  The first step in evaluating patients who present with the symptoms above would be to exclude mechanical obstruction and peptic ulcer disease. All patients should undergo an upper gastrointestinal end\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.80):]\n  Source: Development and psychometric evaluation of the Diabetic Gastroparesis Symptom Severity Diary. > History and Physical\n  Nausea is the most common symptom in gastroparesis. Other common symptoms include vomiting, early satiety, postprandial fullness, and bloating. Vomitus often contains undigested chewed food. Both weig\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  symptoms exacerbated with large fatty meals and alcohol → delayed gastric emptying (diabetic gastroparesis mechanism) → Diabetic Gastroparesis\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess gastric emptying time via a gastric emptying study to confirm the pathophysiological mechanism linking diabetes to symptoms\n  poorly managed diabetes + constipation → diabetic autonomic neuropathy → Neurogenic Motility Disorder (Enteric Nervous System Damage)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for autonomic neuropathy using quantitative sudomotor testing to establish the neurogenic basis for GI motility dysfunction\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
